+
+The added rows have K distribution `{8:48,10:68,12:134,14:33,15:60}`; all have `s=3`. The first is `(n,m)=(36,24)`, `M=(16,34)`, `V=(1,3)`. It passes the stripped Moh numerical tests and is excluded by GGV, so it is **not** a demonstrated missing counterexample datum. At `n<=100`, the counts are 592 versus 658. The complete numerical row list, not just examples, is [core-rows-kmin2.jsonl](../box/census-coverage-20260905/core-rows-kmin2.jsonl).
+
+Default depth counts are `{3:1677,4:10464,5:9746,6:1833}`. All 1,833 height-six rows are at n=192. One is `(192,128)`, `M_2,...,M_6=(-96,-80,-72,-36,190)`, `V_2,...,V_6=(1,24,12,6,3)`, with `(d_2,...,d_6)=(64,32,16,8,4)`. Thus imposing an additional `s<=5` would remove these numerical rows; **the charged code does not do that**. Their survival in this count does not assert realizability.
+
+All 24,063 rows were independently re-evaluated through `Skel`: no duplicates, window failures, full-condition failures, nonintegral major capacity, or failures of the bottom identity `A_1 | (e+d)V_2-1`. `any10()` fails on 4,749 default rows and 277 low-K extras: these remain in the core because the helper is not called. Whole-tree extension, every sibling's bottom test, centre obstruction, and ODE nondegeneracy are further A pruning supplied by the operative screen. There is no claim that the core enforces all algebraic realizability constraints or reproduces Moh's unpublished final program. The six printed p.202 rows are controls, not a proof that no other numerical row should exist.
+
+Core exhaustiveness is also a proof about loops, not an inference from those tests. Given a normalized realized row, its K and coprime reduced ratio occur in the outer loops; its strict gcd chain occurs in `divisor_chains`; its increasing M-values occur in every `Mrec` interval. Starting at the top, its actual multiplicity lies inside each integer V window and satisfies the two-point strict bound. Inductively R and LCM are exactly Def.5.1's product and upper denominator LCM. The all-degree orbit and terminal tests therefore retain that multiplicity. The generator yields the row. No step uses `n<=100` or `s<=5`.
+
+**The operative whole-tree screen.** The charged scope report's implementation is `scope_enum.operative -> opus5_probe.Tree`, with `gate=False`, `ode=True`, `recenter=True`, `capacity=False`, `passport=False`. An independent `full_tree_partition` implementation is used by the Xu driver. Both were replayed against **every one of the 24,063 rows**, with zero difference between their survivor sets, not merely equal totals.
+
+At an internal level j, the branch-specific quantities are
+
+`P=V_{j+1}d_j/d_{j+1}`, `Q=V_{j+1}(n-M_j)/d_{j+1}`, `lo=P/Q=d_j/(n-M_j)`.
+
+These are `deg p`, `deg q`, and the major threshold in Prop.4.6 p.170 / Prop.5.3 p.182. The operative P,Q are not the core's temporary variable named Q in `div9`.
+
+| Operative condition | Necessary justification; class |
+|---|---|
+| Integral nonnegative zero multiplicity `b`, `b congruent P mod A`, `0<=b<=P`; nonzero-orbit multiplicities form a multiset with `P=b+A sum(v)` | **A**, full orbit bookkeeping from p.201's action. Enumerating all b and nondecreasing multisets loses no unordered partition. No multiplicity/partition search cap. |
+| At most `floor((Q-1[b>0])/A)` nonzero orbits in Opus5 | **A**, Prop.4.6(3),(4): q is squarefree and contains every p-root. Each nonzero orbit uses A distinct q-roots, zero uses one if present. The other implementation uses the weaker `floor(Q/A)`, harmless for containment. |
+| Require at least one factor `v>lo` | **A**, Theorem(7) p.200, or Prop.A.3(4) p.205. |
+| Recurse on **every** factor above lo, including an unselected zero sibling | **A**, Theorem(4) p.200 and Prop.5.3. Each actual major child extends. Testing only the recorded path would miss pruning. |
+| The recorded `V_j` path must embed in one such tree | **A**, the realized tree itself supplies an embedding. Other siblings are existentially completed, with no invented equality among different branches. |
+| Every bottom major child satisfies (12) or (13) | **A**, the terminal proof above applies separately to every child. |
+| Reject positive root multiplicity v with `P=Qv` | **A**, the local ODE from Prop.4.6/Prop.A.3. From `P p q' - Q p' q = c p`, at a p-root a of multiplicity v, squarefreeness gives `(P-Qv)q'(a)=c!=0`. Applies to zero and nonzero roots alike. |
+| Reject a still-centred, removable all-zero chain reaching bottom | **A after the explicit C affine removal**, Prop.5.6 pp.188–190 plus the full-Galois centre argument below. The Boolean `danger` becomes permanently false after a non-removable nonzero coefficient. |
+
+The last row cannot be justified merely by identifying all zero **labels** with a zero **centre**. Here is the needed argument. After subtracting the top slope, follow a still-zero major packet. It is invariant under the full Puiseux Galois group over `k((t))`, since all preceding nonintegral coefficients on that path vanish. If its common centre had a first nonzero nonintegral term `a t^e` strictly before the next declared radius, a conjugation moving `t^e` would give two roots in the same zero packet first separated at e. Prop.5.3 p.180 defines the child as the **minimal disc containing that entire packet** of roots of the indicated product of g and approximate roots. Its radius would be e, contradicting the prescribed next radius. This excludes intervening terms, including those in the old 1/L lattice fixed by the *current* increment action.
+
+Induct on the zero path. By Lemma6.1 p.194 and Def.5.1, the radii below the top lie in `[0,1)`, so the only possible integral centre term is the constant, in addition to the already removed slope `a t^-1`. The source automorphism `(x,y)->(x,y-a x-b)` printed on p.190 removes them. A nonzero coefficient at radius zero is removable in exactly this sense; a nonzero coefficient at positive fractional radius is not. Thus a path on which `danger` remains true has bottom general point `pi t^delta_1` after a polynomial affine change. Prop.5.6 then contradicts noncoordinateness/minimality. Multiplicity of the zero factor does not change this packet argument. It makes no claim about coefficients below a **nonzero** fractional ancestor.
+
+This supplies an all-degree proof for the actual centre rejection; disabling it merely restores numerical candidates. The current increment action alone would leave a proof gap when L>1, but the full ancestor action repairs that gap without imposing a new search cap. The optional `capacity` and `passport` flags are false in this screen. In particular the stronger congruence `A | Q-1` and optional passport conditions are not secretly used in obtaining 1,420. Nor are child-top conventions, U-NEGATIVE kills, a descended total-degree cap, or a special `s'=2` Appendix-II shape part of this screen.
+
+**Xu is two distinct screens, not one unexplained label.** The original census-wide `xu_screen.XuBounder` uses **Corollary 5.3**, `IM>=Im`, supported by Theorem5.1 and Theorem4.7 (Xu pp.5,7). It does not apply Cor.7.5 to every row. It optimizes permissive bounds over whole-tree completions embedding the selected path:
+
+`IM_max >= Im_min = 1 + principal_minor_floor + other_minor_floor`.
+
+For a bottom major packet of f-mass `v*m/d_2`, the code's contribution to the upper bound is
+
+`(v*m/d_2) * n/(m+n) * (1-delta_1)`.
+
+Xu Theorem5.1 gives the sum over final major roots. Any further refinement has radius at least `delta_1`, so distributing the fixed mass among later final packets cannot increase this expression. Thus this is an upper bound; no omitted split is assumed attained. Nonzero A-orbits carry A conjugate packets and the recursion sums each distinct packet once.
+
+For an internal minor packet of multiplicity v, the code uses the first possible zero-order radius
+
+`rho_0 = delta_j + [d_j/(n-M_j)]*(1-delta_j)/v`
+
+and contribution `max(0,rho_0-1)`, multiplied by A for a nonzero orbit. Prop.6.1's distribution-detector conclusion pp.190–194 applies while the g-order is negative. Its initial mass gives the maximal slope of that order as the radius increases; splitting can reduce the mass and postpone zero order, never advance it. At least one final minor packet descends from each such disjoint packet. Xu Theorem4.7/Cor.5.3 then license this floor. This is a lower bound, not the exact number or order of every final minor root.
+
+For the principal packet the original implementation uses `v_s-1` only when `u_s=1`, justified by Moh Prop.6.4 pp.198–199, and **zero when `u_s>1`**. That omission is permissive. The minimum minor bound and maximum major bound may come from different completions; this also weakens the screen safely. A realized completion necessarily lies between them, so rejection when the upper bound is smaller than the lower bound is valid. All sums and comparisons use exact rationals. The replay's additional stronger-floor diagnostic is reported separately; it does not define the original conjunction audited here.
+
+The **direct Corollary 7.5** helper is in the snapshotted `box/lib/split_window.py`, lines 344–355. Its actual path goes through `skeleton_view` (183–219), which checks the FS endpoint, characteristic gcd data, `s>2`, and `v_s>u_s>=2`; `genuine_partitions` returns partitions with at least two parts. These are principal-minor face partitions. The helper excludes only
+
+`partition == (1,)*u_s and rho < (v_s+1)/(u_s+1)`.
+
+That is Xu's printed statement on p.11, with proof p.12: the full `u_s`-distinct split, principal minor roots, `u_s>1`, and a **strict** cutoff. It leaves partial partitions and equality untouched. For example, at `(u_s,v_s)=(3,8)`, rho=2 excludes `(1,1,1)` but not `(2,1)`; at `(2,5)`, rho=2 equals the cutoff and is not excluded. [xu75-controls.json](../box/census-coverage-20260905/xu75-controls.json) records strict-below, equality, above, partial-partition, and caller-scope checks. The helper alone does not validate arbitrary inputs (`u_s=1` in particular); its audited caller does. Applying it out of that path without its hypotheses would not inherit this theorem.
+
+The split-window caller records Cor.7.5 as an independent certificate; its G/L face tests determine its chart exclusions. A chart exclusion is not automatically a parent-row exclusion: a principal packet may split otherwise or reach the licensed descent radius. The 1,420-row census does not apply Cor.7.5 indiscriminately to all `u_s>1` rows, and this audit does not add such a rejection.
+
+| Mechanical screen, `n<=200` | Surviving rows | Interpretation |
+|---|---:|---|
+| Core, Kmin=2 | 24,063 | All stripped-list numerical candidates |
+| Core, Kmin=16 | 23,720 | Additional all-degree GGV pruning |
+| `C_FULL_TREE_POLYNOMIAL_ODE` | **1,420** | Same result for both Kmin choices; 686 groups |
+| That screen AND original Xu Cor.5.3 bound | **1,377** | 43 rows removed; 29 whole groups removed |
+| Stronger principal-floor diagnostic | 1,372 | 48 rows removed from 1,420; a different policy |
+
+At `n<=100`, Kmin=2 gives 658 core rows, 20 operative rows, and 17 after original Xu. The exact all-degree replay took about 36 seconds for the core/tree/Xu comparison and has **zero full-tree implementation mismatches**. Individual rows and both bounds are in [op-ft-xu-count.json](../box/census-coverage-20260905/op-ft-xu-count.json). A count of 1,420 for the conjunction would be a population-label error, not a coverage leak: it describes a larger, still safe necessary-condition residue.
+
+**The containment proof closes the requested foundation obligation.** Start with a minimal non-coordinate Keller pair and apply the licensed normalizations and promoted First-Separation lemma. The table of A conditions applies without a degree cap. The explicit loop argument places each of its major-tower rows in the core. The actual factor tree supplies the operative integer partitions; every actual major successor extends and satisfies the terminal equation. Its centre cannot follow a prohibited removable path by Prop.5.6 and the packet argument. Its local ODE forbids every rejected degenerate multiplicity. Finally its actual Xu intersection data lie between the computed bounds. Therefore neither the core nor the original operative conjunction can discard that row. Restricting the normalized degree to `n<=200` yields the finite coverage theorem claimed at the beginning.
+
+The literal p.201 item (4) remains defective wording, but is replaced by a named printed theorem, so it leaves no undecidable implemented restriction. Neither B clause leaks into the code. The 343 low-K extras and 1,833 height-six rows are fully enumerated diagnostics, not counts of realized counterexamples. No repaired enumerator is mathematically required; the charged source was left unchanged. The necessary repair is to its description: **all-degree consequences of the search list, with explicit normalizations and GGV**, not “(1)–(13) implemented verbatim”; and **1,420 before Xu / 1,377 after original Xu**. An unrestricted choice `Kmin>16`, an extra height-five cutoff, or importing UNI/H2 as unconditional would require a new coverage audit.
+
+**FALLACY-v2 and reproducibility.** Printed search normalization was separated from theorem at the exact p.200–201 lines above. Every normalization names a source/target automorphism. The Puiseux action distinguishes the old lattice, branch coefficient, and entire physical packet. Major upper bounds and minor floors are never turned into attainment; a numerical row is never called a realized pair. The parent's `V_{s+1}=d_{s+1}` is not transported to a child. No new exit-price assertion is made, so no charge-basis declaration applies.
