+The last row cannot be justified merely by identifying all zero **labels** with a zero **centre**. Here is the needed argument. After subtracting the top slope, follow a still-zero major packet. It is invariant under the full Puiseux Galois group over `k((t))`, since all preceding nonintegral coefficients on that path vanish. If its common centre had a first nonzero nonintegral term `a t^e` strictly before the next declared radius, a conjugation moving `t^e` would give two roots in the same zero packet first separated at e. Prop.5.3 p.180 defines the child as the **minimal disc containing that entire packet** of roots of the indicated product of g and approximate roots. Its radius would be e, contradicting the prescribed next radius. This excludes intervening terms, including those in the old 1/L lattice fixed by the *current* increment action.
+
+Induct on the zero path. By Lemma6.1 p.194 and Def.5.1, the radii below the top lie in `[0,1)`, so the only possible integral centre term is the constant, in addition to the already removed slope `a t^-1`. The source automorphism `(x,y)->(x,y-a x-b)` printed on p.190 removes them. A nonzero coefficient at radius zero is removable in exactly this sense; a nonzero coefficient at positive fractional radius is not. Thus a path on which `danger` remains true has bottom general point `pi t^delta_1` after a polynomial affine change. Prop.5.6 then contradicts noncoordinateness/minimality. Multiplicity of the zero factor does not change this packet argument. It makes no claim about coefficients below a **nonzero** fractional ancestor.
